2.3. Functional Requirements 
2.3. Functional Requirements

The following functional requirements define the main functions of the AR-IMMS (Augmented Reality Infrastructure Monitoring and Maintenance System). Each requirement is assigned a unique identifier (FR01–FR18) to support requirement traceability, Use Case Specifications, and the Requirement Traceability Matrix (RTM).

2.3.1. Authentication

FR01 – Login
The system shall allow Administrators, Operators, and Technicians to log in using valid credentials.

FR02 – Logout
The system shall allow authenticated users to securely log out of the system.

FR03 – Password Change
The system shall allow authenticated users to change their passwords.

2.3.2. User and Role Management

FR04 – Manage User Accounts
The system shall allow the Administrator to create, view, update, and delete user accounts.

FR05 – Manage User Roles and Permissions
The system shall allow the Administrator to assign and manage roles and permissions for Administrators, Operators, and Technicians.

2.3.3. Infrastructure Management

FR06 – Manage Infrastructure
The system shall allow the Administrator to manage the infrastructure hierarchy consisting of Site, Room, Rack, Server, and Container.

FR07 – View Infrastructure Information
The system shall allow Administrators, Operators, and Technicians to view infrastructure information and the current status of monitored components.

2.3.4. Telemetry Management

FR08 – Collect and Send Telemetry
The system shall allow the Collector to collect real-time CPU, RAM, Disk, and Network metrics from monitored infrastructure components and send the collected telemetry data to the Backend.

FR09 – Store and View Telemetry
The system shall store received telemetry data and allow authorized users to view historical and current telemetry information.

2.3.5. Alert Management

FR10 – Detect Anomalies
The system shall automatically detect abnormal infrastructure conditions based on predefined thresholds.

FR11 – Manage Alert Lifecycle
The system shall allow authorized users to view and manage alerts through the following lifecycle:

OPEN → ACKNOWLEDGED → RESOLVED → CLOSED

2.3.6. Incident Management

FR12 – Manage Incident
The system shall allow authorized users to create and manage incidents when an alert requires formal investigation or handling.

2.3.7. Ticket Management

FR13 – Create and Assign Ticket
The system shall allow authorized users to create maintenance tickets and assign them to Technicians.

FR14 – Manage Ticket Status
The system shall allow authorized users to update ticket status through the following lifecycle:

OPEN → IN_PROGRESS → RESOLVED → CLOSED

2.3.8. Maintenance Management

FR15 – Record Maintenance
The system shall allow Technicians to record maintenance results, including maintenance description, time, and technician information.

2.3.9. Digital Twin

FR16 – Synchronize Digital Twin
The system shall synchronize the current status of physical infrastructure devices with their corresponding digital representations based on updated infrastructure and telemetry data.

2.3.10. Augmented Reality

FR17 – Identify Device Using AR
The system shall allow Technicians to scan QR codes or ArUco markers to identify physical infrastructure devices.

FR18 – View Device Information Using AR
The system shall allow Technicians to view device information, telemetry, alerts, and tickets through the Mobile AR interface.
2.6. Use Case Diagram — BẢN CHỐT
2.6. Use Case Diagram

The Use Case Diagram provides an overall view of the interactions between the main actors and the AR-IMMS system. It identifies the major functions provided by the system and describes how each actor interacts with these functions.

The AR-IMMS system has four main actors: Administrator, Operator, Technician, and Collector.

The Administrator is responsible for managing user accounts, roles and permissions, infrastructure information, and system activities. The Administrator can also monitor infrastructure and manage alerts, incidents, and tickets according to assigned permissions.

The Operator is responsible for monitoring infrastructure, viewing telemetry data, and handling alerts, incidents, and tickets during daily infrastructure operations.

The Technician is responsible for monitoring infrastructure, reviewing telemetry data, handling alerts and incidents, processing maintenance tickets, performing maintenance activities, and using Mobile AR to identify devices and view device information.

The Collector is an external agent responsible for collecting infrastructure metrics from monitored devices and transmitting telemetry data to the Backend.

The main use cases of AR-IMMS are derived from the functional requirements defined in Section 2.3.

